First of all, even though a popular mod manager is easier to learn, an alternative mod organizer is a better choice. It installs mods in a separate folder from game files, so you will have fewer issues if your mod setup doesn't work. That said, you can start modding with the easier manager if you want at first, just think about switching over to the alternative if you start installing lots of mods.

Something really important: use a load order sorting tool to organize your mod load order after having installed a few of them.

To start, install a script extender, it's required for most mods now. Then, a user interface mod should be your first mod, it's a requirement for many other mods and has a better mouse/keyboard interface. If you want to keep the classic menus (to play with a controller for instance), install a mod that allows you to do that.

Now, if you've got the downloadable content, install an unofficial patch. It's a mod that has a lot of fixes for glitches and problems that were not addressed by the original developers. If you only have the base game, or not all the downloadable content, install the other versions.

If you're playing with mouse and keyboard, install mods that improve dialogue and message box controls to make your life easier navigating the menus and selecting options.

Now, some mods to improve the game overall:

- A mod that allows you to show or hide and customize the HUD;

- A camera mod that lets you see your character's body in first person;

- A killmove customization mod;

- A customizable camera options mod;

- A mod that removes the spinning death animation NPCs make when dying;

- A main menu mod that shows more info about the save you're loading;

- A follower framework mod that allows you to have a huge range of options for followers;

- A dialogue expansion mod for followers;

- A quest timing mod that gives you control over a lot of quests so that you can delay them or start them earlier;

- A mod that makes non-fighter NPCs flee when attacked by dragons, and another that does the same for vampire attacks.

Some other mods I like:

- A door knocking mod that allows you to knock on NPCs' doors and they can let you in (if they want to), which is super useful sometimes;

- A quick loot mod that allows you to loot items from containers or corpses without opening the menu, with a reskin that has a better interface as well;

- A combat enhancement mod that improves enemy AI and works with both the base game and combat mods, one of my favorites;

- A mod that lets you see all the weapons you have equipped on your character;

- An alternate start mod that lets you configure tons of options (like being a special character or not) and your starting location, or even make everything random.

Anyway, these are some great mods, not all essentials, but they should improve on the base game.

